De Quervain's Tenosynovitis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Radial styloid pain, Exacerbation with thumb/wrist movement, Positive Finkelstein's test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-017
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of wrist pain rendering the patient entirely unable to use their dominant hand for basic infant care and self-care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and infant child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a new mother experiencing an agonizing, sharp pain in the dominant wrist causing her to drop items and making it physically impossible to safely lift her infant. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute scaphoid fracture or severe joint infection, and to provide immediate mechanical immobilization and pain relief to restore functional capacity and ensure the safety of the dependent child prior to morning services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "A shooting pain goes up my thumb every time I try to pick up my baby; I almost dropped him this afternoon because my wrist gave way."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right radial wrist pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of a dull, aching pain at the base of the thumb developing over the last 3 weeks since increasing infant lifting; progressed to a sharp, catching pain two days ago; culminated today in an excruciating, stabbing pain whenever attempting to grip or pinch, causing sudden weakness. Patient reports extreme difficulty performing repetitive tasks and describes a catching, snapping sensation when moving the thumb.
Functional Status: Profoundly impaired dominant hand function; unable to safely lift or support her infant.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate infant safety risk due to sudden failure of grip strength.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, exhausted, visually guarding the right wrist and holding it against the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Right wrist shows palpable swelling and thickening specifically over the first dorsal compartment. Exquisite point tenderness located directly over the radial styloid process and the abductor pollicis longus/extensor pollicis brevis tendons. Distinctly positive Finkelstein's test: grasping the thumb within a fist and passively ulnar deviating the wrist produces a sharp, reproducible pain that the patient describes as "electric." Sensation and pulses are intact. Weakness in pinch grip strength is noted, clearly secondary to pain inhibition rather than primary motor deficit. No erythema or warmth spreading up the arm.
Environmental Observations: Bassinet and changing table are positioned at heights requiring awkward, repetitive lifting mechanics.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol 1g taken with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute De Quervain's Tenosynovitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Scaphoid Fracture - Ruled out by the absence of a specific traumatic fall onto an outstretched hand and lack of distinct tenderness deep within the anatomical snuffbox compared to the more proximal tendon sheath.

Radiocarpal Osteoarthritis - Ruled out by the specific tendon-driven pain mechanism, absence of generalized joint line tenderness, and lack of bony crepitus on global wrist motion.

Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - Ruled out by the absence of palmar numbness/tingling in the median nerve distribution and negative Tinel's/Phalen's signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient, a new mother, presented with an acute, functionally incapacitating flare of radial wrist tenosynovitis, highly characteristic of De Quervain's given the repetitive biomechanical demands of lifting an infant. The critical clinical issue was the immediate safety risk posed by her sudden inability to securely grip or lift her child. Delaying assessment would have subjected the patient to unmanageable pain, severe distress regarding infant care, and potential further tendon microtrauma. Urgent after-hours intervention allowed for diagnostic confirmation, exclusion of a scaphoid fracture, and the immediate application of a specialized thumb-spica splint to mechanically offload the inflamed tendons, restoring a baseline level of safe function.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food for 7 days to aggressively manage acute tenosynovial inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Wear the provided thumb-spica splint continuously, especially when lifting the infant. Consequence: The splint physically blocks the painful thumb movement, allowing the patient to lift confidently without the sudden, agonizing pain spikes that cause dropping (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply an ice pack wrapped in a thin towel directly to the thumb base for 15 minutes after feeding or changing the baby. Consequence: Immediate constriction of the inflamed tissues, providing a soothing, numbing effect that drastically reduces the lingering, throbbing ache (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a rigid thumb-spica splint to the right hand.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days for consideration of a targeted corticosteroid injection if oral NSAIDs and splinting fail to provide adequate relief.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the wrist becomes intensely red, hot, or diffusely swollen, if a high fever develops, or if there is a sudden inability to move the fingers at all.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical. The condition is mechanically driven by repetitive strain. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will subside with the splint and anti-inflammatories over 5-7 days. However, the underlying tendon thickening requires strict biomechanical modification (scooping the baby rather than lifting under the arms) to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the wrist becomes hot and red, if you develop a fever, or if the pain becomes completely unmanageable despite the splint and medications.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The two tendons that pull your thumb out and back are trapped in a tight tunnel at your wrist. Repetitive lifting has made them swell, causing severe friction. The thumb-spica splint locks the thumb in place to completely stop that friction, while the ibuprofen reduces the swelling inside the tunnel.
Actionable Steps: Do not take the splint off for lifting tasks. Change your lifting technique: slide your hands under the baby's bottom and head to "scoop" them up, rather than using your thumbs like hooks under their armpits.

